# Production MCP (not Atlassian)

Ticket, timer, and comment work for **Cardinal Construction** goes through the
**`user-wtn-production`** MCP server only.

**Do not** use the Atlassian MCP (Jira/Confluence) for this repo. When the user says
"work on ticket #N", "close the ticket", "create a ticket", or similar, they mean
**CC tickets** on `user-wtn-production` — not Jira issues.

## Default production context

| What | Value |
|------|-------|
| MCP server | `user-wtn-production` |
| Project | **Cardinal Construction** (`project_id: 6`) |
| Project client | Ted Bennett (`client_id: 6`) |
| Acting admin | call `whoami` to confirm the `user_id` |

If a call 404s or these ids look wrong, re-resolve them with `list-projects` /
`get-project` instead of guessing.

## When to create a ticket

If we are doing **non-trivial work in this repo** (feature, bug fix, refactor,
investigation with code changes) and there is no existing ticket for it, **propose
creating one** on Cardinal Construction before or as work starts. Skip for one-line fixes,
docs-only nits, or when the user explicitly says no ticket.

Use `create-ticket` with `project_id: 6`, assigning the admin returned by
`whoami` unless told otherwise.

## Ticket lifecycle

Drive the lifecycle from the SDLC workflows, not from the low-level tools. Use the
`/start-ticket` and `/end-ticket` MCP prompts where the host expands them; where it
does not (Cursor Cloud Agents today), call the **`sdlc-start-ticket`** /
**`sdlc-end-ticket`** tools and follow the steps they return. Both entry points render
the same workflow text, so the policy is identical either way — notably that shipping
sets `awaiting_client`, never `closed`.

1. **Start** — `whoami`, then `get-ticket` / `list-tickets` to load context.
2. **Timer** — `start-timer` when implementation begins (`ticket_id` + `project_id`).
3. **Progress** — `update-ticket` (`in_progress`, `percent_complete`) as work advances.
4. **Ship notes** (`/end-ticket`) — two comments when detail is worth keeping:
   - **Client** (`is_internal: false`): outcome-first, plain language. No PR numbers,
     commits, branches, CI links, or file paths.
   - **Internal** (`is_internal: true`, optional): engineering detail for later review
     or a client call (PR/commit, deploy path, files, root cause). Skip if trivial.
5. **Ship** — `update-ticket` with `status: awaiting_client`,
   `percent_complete: 100`, and `assigned_to_user_id` set to the project client
   (`project.client.id` from `get-ticket`). Use `status: closed` only when the user explicitly asks to
   close. Then `stop-timer` with `ticket_id` (see **Timer** below).

Comments are client-visible by default; pass `is_internal: true` for admin-only notes,
including the optional ship engineering log.

## Timer

- Call `start-timer` when implementation on a ticket begins. Starting a timer does
  **not** stop other running timers for this admin or any other admin.
- When stopping, pass `ticket_id` (or `time_entry_id`) so only this ticket's timer
  is closed — other concurrent timers stay running.
- **Do not** call `stop-timer` until the full ship workflow is done: changes accepted,
  committed, pushed, and deployed (when deploy was requested) — **or** until the user
  explicitly says to stop.
- Shipping the ticket and adding ship notes may happen before deploy; keep the timer
  running until deploy completes when deploy is part of the workflow.

## MCP vs local dev

| Task | Use |
|------|-----|
| Tickets, timers, comments | `user-wtn-production` MCP |
| Code, tests, migrations | This repo + `./cc` (see [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md)) |
| Production DB / production data | MCP or SSH — **never** `./cc tinker` |

Local `./cc` artisan, tinker, and test commands hit the **dev** database only.

## Ship / deploy

**Deploy mode: `manual`.** There is no GitHub Actions (or other CI) deploy workflow
in this repo. Shipping needs an explicit deploy after the push, and only with the
user's go-ahead. Default branch is `master` (`agent_starting_ref` on the CC project).

Until a documented deploy path lands in-repo (scripts + ADR), ask the user how this
host is updated before attempting any production change. Do **not** invent a deploy
command.
